# Cursor Rules for miataru iOS App

## Project Context
This is an open-source iOS application for privacy-friendly location tracking, sharing, and device management. Built with SwiftUI and modern iOS frameworks (iOS 18 and iOS 26).

## Code Style & Standards
- Use SwiftUI best practices
- Follow iOS Human Interface Guidelines
- Maintain privacy-first approach
- Use English for all code comments and strings
- Implement proper error handling and battery optimization

## Architecture Guidelines
- Use ObservableObject and @Published for state management
- Keep views lightweight and delegate business logic to managers
- Implement proper MVVM pattern
- Use UserDefaults for settings persistence
- Handle location permissions gracefully

## Key Components
- LocationManager: Handles location tracking and permissions
- SettingsManager: Manages user preferences
- Device management and QR code functionality
- Multi-platform support (iPhone, iPad, Mac)

## Development Workflow
- Test on multiple device types
- Consider battery optimization
- Maintain privacy compliance
- Follow iOS app store guidelines

## Common Patterns
- Use @StateObject for view models
- Implement proper onboarding flow
- Handle background/foreground transitions
- Manage device identifiers securely

## Localization Guidelines

### String Localization
**ALWAYS** use `NSLocalizedString` for all user-facing text strings with proper English descriptions.

#### ✅ Correct Implementation
```swift
Text(NSLocalizedString("no_groups_available_create_new", comment: "No groups available. Create a new group to get started."))
```

#### ❌ Incorrect Implementation
```swift
Text("No groups available. Create a new group to get started.")
```

### Localization Key Naming Convention
- Use descriptive, lowercase keys with underscores
- Follow the pattern: `context_action_description`
- Examples:
  - `group_empty_add_devices_message`
  - `no_groups_available_create_new`
  - `edit_device`
  - `delete_group`

### Comment Guidelines
- Provide clear, descriptive English text in the comment parameter
- Use proper English grammar and punctuation
- Make the comment self-explanatory for translators
- Avoid technical jargon in comments

### Adding New Localized Strings
1. Add the `NSLocalizedString` call in your Swift code
2. Add the key-value pair to `Localizable.xcstrings`
3. Provide translations for all supported languages (en, de, ja)

## File Naming Conventions

### Directory Structure
```
miataru/
├── views/
│   ├── iPhone/
│   ├── iPad/
│   ├── Mac/
│   └── Common/
├── LocationManagers/
├── SettingsManagers/
└── Libraries/
```

### File Naming
- iPhone views: `iPhone_ViewName.swift`
- iPad views: `iPad_ViewName.swift`
- Mac views: `Mac_ViewName.swift`
- Common/Shared views: `ViewName.swift`
- Group related files in descriptive directories
- Use clear, descriptive names for files
- Follow the established pattern for platform-specific views

## Copyright Header Requirements

### Mandatory Copyright Header
**ALWAYS** add the following copyright header to the top of every new Swift file:

```swift
/*
 * Copyright (c) 2013-2025, Daniel Kirstenpfad, www.miataru.com
 *
 * [FileName].swift
 * miataru
 *
 * Created by Daniel Kirstenpfad on [CurrentDate].
 */
```

#### ✅ Correct Implementation
```swift
/*
 * Copyright (c) 2013-2025, Daniel Kirstenpfad, www.miataru.com
 *
 * MyNewView.swift
 * miataru
 *
 * Created by Daniel Kirstenpfad on 2025-01-25.
 */

import SwiftUI
// ... rest of the file content
```

#### ❌ Incorrect Implementation
```swift
import SwiftUI
// Missing copyright header
// ... rest of the file content
```

### Header Guidelines
- Place the header at the very top of the file, before any imports
- Replace `[FileName]` with the actual filename (e.g., `MyNewView.swift`)
- Replace `[CurrentDate]` with the current date in format YYYY-MM-DD (e.g., 2025-01-25)
- Keep the exact copyright text and format
- The header must be the first content in every Swift file

## Architecture Patterns

### MVVM with SwiftUI
- Use `@StateObject` for ViewModels
- Keep business logic separate from UI code
- Use `@Published` properties for reactive updates

### Dependency Injection
- Use `@EnvironmentObject` for shared services
- Pass dependencies through initializers when appropriate
- Keep dependencies minimal and focused

### State Management
- Use local `@State` for view-specific state
- Use `@ObservedObject` for shared state
- Consider using `@StateObject` for complex state objects

### Property Wrappers
- Use `@StateObject` for objects that are created and owned by the view
- Use `@ObservedObject` for objects passed in from parent views
- Use `@EnvironmentObject` for objects injected through the environment
- Use `@State` for simple value types owned by the view

## Testing Guidelines

### Unit Tests
- Test business logic separately from UI
- Mock dependencies for isolated testing
- Use descriptive test names that explain the scenario

### UI Tests
- Test user workflows end-to-end
- Use accessibility identifiers for reliable element selection
- Test on different device sizes when possible

## Common Patterns

### Error Handling
```swift
private func showErrorOverlay(_ debugMessage: String, _ userMessage: String) {
    print("Error: \(debugMessage)")
    errorOverlayManager.show(message: userMessage)
}
```

### API Calls
```swift
Task { 
    await fetchAllLocations() 
}
```

### Animation
```swift
withAnimation(.easeInOut(duration: 0.5)) {
    cameraPosition = .region(newRegion)
}
```

## Best Practices

### Performance
- Use `LazyVStack` and `LazyHStack` for large lists
- Avoid expensive operations in `body` computed property
- Use `@State` sparingly and only for view-specific state

### Memory Management
- Cancel timers and publishers in `onDisappear`
- Use weak references when appropriate
- Avoid retain cycles in closures

### Accessibility
- Provide meaningful labels for interactive elements
- Use semantic colors and fonts
- Test with VoiceOver and other accessibility features

### Variable Naming
- Use camelCase for variables and functions
- Use descriptive names that explain the purpose
- Avoid abbreviations unless they are widely understood

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Copyright header is present and correctly formatted
- [ ] All user-facing strings use `NSLocalizedString`
- [ ] Proper error handling implemented
- [ ] Memory leaks prevented (timers, publishers cancelled)
- [ ] Code follows established naming conventions
- [ ] Appropriate property wrappers used
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed
- [ ] Accessibility features implemented

## Memories

### Copyright Header Requirements
- When new .swift files are created in the project, the cursor rules should automatically insert the standard copyright header. The date field in the header should always be set to the current date.

### Commit Message Language
- The user prefers commit messages to be written in English.

### Changelog on Commit
- When you commit code or project changes, extend `miataru/CHANGELOG.md` at the top with the version from the main app’s `MARKETING_VERSION` (in `miataru.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj`; use the value for the miataru target, not 1.0). If that version is not yet at the top of the changelog, add a new “version X.Y” section at the top and list bullet-point summaries of the changes below it. Include the CHANGELOG update in the same commit.

### String Localization
- The user prefers that all strings be implemented as NSLocalizedString with correct English descriptions.

